Doesn't look that different to me, and that's exactly what the MAG said
before the relationship between XL and difficulty, and the futility of
lingering on cleared levels, was explained to you _over and over_. Putting
words into your mouth? Hardly. I'm taking them directly from your own
postings.

>Perhaps your memory is faulty - I suggest you reread the "Starting
>Strategy" section of
>  http://homepage.mac.com/mhjohnson/mag-r343.html

Yes, I'm aware you've revised it to be hopelessly vague (and for some
reason not call the protection racket by the name every other rgrn poster
calls it by [1]). Determining the exact difference between the "conservative",
"explorer", and "balanced" methods (let alone the "broad range of options"
between three strategies that say much the same thing in a meandering sort
of way) is left as an exercise to the reader.

That whole tranch of waffle ("first and foremost - survive" - no shit,
Sherlock [2]) could be ripped out and replaced with a few simple
observations. Lingering on cleared levels is bad because monster
difficulty is proportional to the average of XL and DL (a fact which is
central to understanding the early game, and shamefully glossed over by
this section of the MAG); fluffing up the pet does not increase difficulty,
but leaves you vulnerable if separated from it; your class makes a big
difference to how much your own combat effectiveness increases when you
gain a level. 

>To clarify - the main point I make is to be flexible in your game
>play, adapting your technique to the situation you find yourself in.

No, you don't. You steer fairly clear of making any recommendation at all
in that section, which is about as useful in a new player guide as a
chocolate fireguard.

>May I also recommend you follow what you said almost five years ago...
>>Actually presently I was kind of taking it to silence, since I think I've
>>said everything that can be said.
>Please do so again.

Well, that depends on two things; whether we get the same idea of
lingering on cleared levels coming round again, and whether you can resist
the urge to bite at a one-line throwaway comment when the MAG still
staggers between painful incoherence and outright foolishness.

[1] Particularly odd when the guide later refers to the protection racket
by its usual name - albeit with no real attempt to explain _how_ one is
supposed to make use of donations to priest at low XL. Presumably because
the racket was half-explained under a funny name earlier?

[2] This is quite typical of the MAG in that that statement of the
blindingly obvious leaves you feeling the lack of the useful observation
about the game it stabs at and misses. What ought to be said there is that
NetHack characters are prone to infant mortality; the early game is the
most dangerous, and a common trap is to save something to make the game
easier later. An early scroll of genocide should not be spent on liches if
ants are likely to carry you off. But - alas - squandering resources you
didn't need to survive is its own trap.
